    description: "A zygote is the very first cell of a new living thing, formed the moment a sperm cell and an egg cell join together during fertilization. This single cell carries a complete set of genetic instructions, half from each parent, combined into one new and unique blueprint. Although it starts as just one cell, the zygote does not stay that way for long. Within hours, it begins to divide, first into two cells, then four, then eight, doubling again and again in a carefully timed sequence. As division continues, the cells begin to specialize, eventually forming the distinct tissues and organs of a complete organism. In humans, this early stage of rapid division happens while the zygote travels toward the uterus, before it implants and continues developing into an embryo. The same basic process plays out across the animal kingdom, from insects to whales, even though the timeline and details differ enormously between species. The zygote is a useful reminder that every complex living creature, no matter how large or intricate, begins from this same simple starting point: a single cell containing everything needed to build a life."
